                <pb facs="0610" n="494" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg494"/>

                <head>Wreck of the British vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Titanic;</hi> consular
                    disposition of effects of the deceased; recognition of the services of Captain
                        Rostron.<anchor n="1" xml:id="ch51-f1"/>
                </head>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Note</hi>.—At 10.13 p.m. (New York time) April 14,
                    1912, the White Star liner <hi rendition="#italic">Titanic</hi>, west bound,
                    with 2,201 persons aboard, struck an iceberg while steaming at from 22 to 24
                    miles per hour, at latitude North 41.46, longitude West 50.14, near Cape Race,
                    and foundered two hours and forty minutes later.</p>
                <p>The President and the Secretary of State received on April 15 and 16 expressions
                    of condolence from the Governments of Argentina, Belgium, Bolivia, Brazil, Cuba,
                    Dominican Republic, France, Great Britain (and the British Colonies), Guatemala,
                    Honduras, Italy, Japan, Mexico, Nicaragua, Panama, Persia, Peru, Portugal (and
                    Lourenco Marquez), Russia, Salvador, Siam, Spain, Sweden, Turkey, Uruguay and
                    Venezuela.</p>
                <p>Certain correspondence ensued relating to the jurisdiction of American consuls in
                    regard to the transportation of bodies, as follows.</p>








